WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.570
Today, we are going to talk about this.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.490
It is without doubt

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:10.860
the most beautiful aircraft

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.800
ever to evolve from the mind of man.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.370
Concord retired in 2003,

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:17.740
ever since then we've been stuck traveling

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:19.410
at regular old speeds

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:23.510
but there is a company
in Colorado called Boom.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:27.220
They've raised more than
$150 million to build

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:31.400
the first privately funded
commercial supersonic plane.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.440
The company was started by
a man named Blake Scholl.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.310
He came from the internet
and software world

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.530
but decided he was gonna be the guy

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.720
who would make this machine possible.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.070
And so they'd been at it for a few years.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.070
Overture is the goal,

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:50.010
and before that they are building a plane

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.160
that's about a third
the size of this thing,

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.410
which is called XB-1,
and Boom is unveiling it

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.990
for the first time, and we
get the first look at it.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:01.120
So let's head to Colorado
and hear from Blake

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.240
about Boom's progress, and with that,

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:06.153
off we go to Colorado.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:10.900
So I've been following
you guys since what?

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.240
Since like 2015, 2016?

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:14.837
I think it was early 2016, yeah.

00:01:14.837 --> 00:01:18.010
Give me the whole thesis behind Boom

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:19.810
and what you guys are trying to do.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:22.120
It's been a really half a century

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:23.810
since we've had a speedup in air travel

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:27.010
when we went from props
to jets in the late 1950s,

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:29.120
and in the 1960s and 1970s,

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.520
we made the leap to
supersonic with Concorde,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.220
but we never made high-speed
travel mainstream,

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:37.120
and in fact, when Concorde
shut down about 20 years ago,

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:40.040
there was really no plan to
pick up where it left off.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:41.550
And so that's what we're doing at Boom,

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.470
we're building high-speed travel

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.130
that can be available to
more people than ever before.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.730
So I think New York to London
in three-and-a-half hours

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:50.210
instead of six-and-a-half,

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:53.780
think the West Coast
to a Tokyo in as little

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.020
as four-and-a-half hours.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.120
Think of Overture, which is our airliner,

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.040
as chopping off the
front cabin of a Boeing

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.500
or Airbus wide body and
making it go really fast.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.200
So it's got between 65 and 85 seats

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.980
and we'll get you there in
wheels up to wheels down

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.860
in about half the time it takes today.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.030
If you fly XB-1 next year,

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:16.270
that would be the first
private supersonic plane.

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:17.530
Is that right?

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.980
That's right, when we fly XB-1 next year,

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.560
it'll be the first independently developed

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:22.970
supersonic jet ever.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:24.590
Okay.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:26.340
Tell me just a little bit
about your background,

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.410
and in retrospect, do you
think that's been a service

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:30.630
to what you guys are trying to do?

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.430
I went to school for computer science.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.280
I started my career at Amazon

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:36.490
doing automation of online marketing

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:39.170
and as a software engineer,
and then as a leader,

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.450
but I've loved airplanes
since I was a kid.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:42.420
You fly, right?

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.460
I've been flying for fun since college.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:45.930
I'm one of those people that you know,

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.463
you let me behind the
controls of an airplane

00:02:47.463 --> 00:02:49.610
and, you gotta wrench them outta my hands

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:52.300
'cause it's so much fun,

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.720
and it's such a just
wonderfully human thing to do,

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:59.120
and technical and
physical at the same time.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.250
But I've been watching
the supersonic space

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:02.580
since I was in my mid-twenties

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.360
and I sort of gave myself a
life goal of going Mach two,

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:09.300
and one day I wanted to have
my passion for aerospace

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.370
intersect with what I
was actually working on,

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.110
and had no idea when that would happen,

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.300
or how it would happen,
but I feel very lucky

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.080
to be able to do Boom.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:19.520
Why has it been since 2003,

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:23.380
that nobody tried to do this again?

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:24.850
We're now at the intersection

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:27.370
of what is technologically possible

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:29.580
and what there is a
sufficient market demand for,

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.820
so since Concorde retired in 03,

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:33.690
there's been a
two-and-a-half fold increase

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.217
in international travel,

00:03:35.217 --> 00:03:37.897
and that creates a lot more
markets for supersonic.

00:03:37.897 --> 00:03:41.560
But at the same time, we've
had all kinds of new technology

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:42.980
that didn't exist in Concorde's day.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.690
We've gone from aluminum
to carbon fiber composites.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.130
We've gone from loud, inefficient,

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:52.430
environmentally-unfriendly
turbo jet engines

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:54.580
to turbofan engines that are much quieter,

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.230
much more fuel efficient.

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:58.140
And when you put together
all the new technology,

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.590
aerodynamics, materials, propulsion,

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:02.020
you can now build an aircraft

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:04.710
that's significantly more
efficient than Concorde,

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.090
which means it can be available

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:07.430
to tens of millions of people

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:09.980
at fares kinda like what you
pay in business class today.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:12.630
So you guys are doing this
in this super methodical way.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:16.470
You're unveiling right now, this XB-1,

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.620
which is your first plane,
and tell me about it.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:22.340
I mean, it's basically a third of the size

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:24.900
of where you're hoping
to go with Overture,

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.900
which will be your first commercial plane.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.480
So XB-1 is our, think of it as our like

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.500
baby prototype for Overture.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.430
We call it Baby Boom for fun.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:38.360
And it'll go supersonic,
it'll be history's first

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:40.720
independently developed supersonic jet.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.410
And you know, for those
of you who are familiar

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:44.210
with the early days of SpaceX,

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:46.610
it's in many ways,
analogous to the Falcon 1.

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:48.830
Rather than going in building Overture,

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:51.250
we decided it was prudent to
take all of our best ideas

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:54.720
for that airplane and then
go sub-scale, and do design,

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.360
build, fly, learn on a
representative aircraft

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:02.940
that is human rated, that
actually carries a living pilot.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.950
So we'll be flying XB-1 and
just setting speed records

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:10.830
in 2021, so next year, and
then as XB-1 goes supersonic

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.710
around the end of next year,
we're gonna pass the torch

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.550
from XB-1 to Overture.

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:20.120
And so all the things we've
learned in flight test of XB-1

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:22.090
will inform the final design of Overture,

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:25.360
we're gonna press enter on that
around the end of next year,

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:27.980
and then start signing up
the rest of our supplier team

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:31.080
for Overture, do the detailed design,

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:34.440
and in 2025, so about five years from now,

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:37.120
we will be with Overture
where we are with XB-1 today,

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:40.820
where we're rolling out the
first assembled airplane,

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:42.480
and then we'll be in flight test

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:44.640
starting in 2026 with Overture.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.340
So six years from now you're gonna look up

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:50.250
and you'll see Overture
aircraft you know, in the skies

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:52.180
setting speed records.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:53.790
And so we'll be carrying
our first passengers

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:55.550
by the end of the decade.

00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:59.453
Cool, well, I think we
can actually sort of like

00:05:59.453 --> 00:06:02.050
have a peek down on the
factory floor, right?

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:04.530
With XB-1 kinda coming together.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.280
Yeah, let's go take a look at it.

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:11.850
Here we are overlooking
XB-1 in its assembly hangar,

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:15.870
and this is a 67-foot-long aircraft,

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:17.900
so about one third of
the scale of Overture,

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:20.520
which will be a bit
more than 200 feet long.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:23.963
And over here, we've got
about a 21-foot wingspan.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:28.811
So this hangar that we're
in here is perfect for XB-1,

00:06:28.811 --> 00:06:30.470
but Overture wouldn't
even fit in the building.

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:32.860
So we're gonna move here
in a couple of years

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:35.680
as we start building the first Overture.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:38.390
So the last few components are going on,

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:41.780
and then we're gonna lower the
aircraft down onto a field,

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:45.220
and then we'll be ready to
unveil the aircraft to the world.

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.630
How many people do you guys have working

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:48.973
on the plane right now?

00:06:49.871 --> 00:06:52.023
I've got about 140 people in the company,

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:56.220
of which about 100 are working on XB-1

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:57.940
either physically putting it together,

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:00.970
or finishing the design
and the engineering of it.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:03.740
You can see here, we've
got the main landing gear.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:07.421
That's designed to take
200,000 pounds of load,

00:07:07.421 --> 00:07:10.653
we can provide even in a
relatively rough landing.

00:07:10.653 --> 00:07:13.480
And up above it, this is the intake.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:17.220
So XB-1 and Overture has
just three engines in it.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:20.620
We've got one on each
side underneath the wing,

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:23.880
and then we've got a third one
in the tail with that intake

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:26.980
that's mounted dorsal on
the back of the airplane.

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:28.970
So we've built this deck

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:31.020
so the airplane could be accessible,

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:32.657
offering control systems.

00:07:32.657 --> 00:07:34.740
And we call it the party deck.

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:35.573
Building an airplane,

00:07:35.573 --> 00:07:37.960
it's like building an
iceberg from the bottom up.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:41.700
Pilot sits here, but you
can get a sense that yeah,

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.990
the visibility over this
nose isn't gonna be great,

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:47.290
and that's why we
brought the camera system

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:49.750
that allows the pilot basically,
to have a virtual window

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:51.650
that goes through the nose of the aircraft

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:53.720
and they can see the runway
better than they could

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:55.110
on a typical subsonic airplane.

00:07:55.110 --> 00:08:00.110
You can see that we've got
the nose cone is on now,

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:03.560
and we've got that
silver thing sticking out

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:05.460
as the flight test boom.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:09.670
So that's a piece of equipment
that measures airflow,

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.360
air speed, air pressure,

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:15.660
temperature well outside
of the interference

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:16.493
of the airplane itself.

00:08:16.493 --> 00:08:19.160
We kind of reach forward
to get a clean measurement,

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:20.980
and then we use that to
calibrate the sensors

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:21.840
that are out there for you.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:24.850
There are many, many, many
sensors throughout this,

00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:28.470
and then the whole airplane
has a real-time data link

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:30.380
down to the ground, a 10-megabit link,

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:32.580
and so we've got real-time video feed

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:35.553
plus all of the sensors
real-time off the aircraft,

00:08:35.553 --> 00:08:37.630
down to the control room.

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:40.960
So we've got carbon fiber composite wings,

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:44.470
and you'll notice that the
wings are relatively small

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:45.800
compared to typical airliner wings.

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:49.620
So that's one of the artifacts
of a high-Mach design

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:52.770
is you have relatively less wingspan,

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:55.990
and that, but we've got so
much airflow over the wing

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:57.960
so you can still get all
of the lift you need.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:01.980
And then the trick to making
it also work at low speed,

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:04.900
is you come in at a
very nose-high attitude

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:07.360
called a high-angle of
attack, and at that point,

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:10.450
the whole aircraft is in
something called vortex lift,

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:12.964
where basically you get a
vortex to generate off the nose,

00:09:12.964 --> 00:09:15.367
you get a vortex generated off
the leading edge of the wing,

00:09:16.767 --> 00:09:18.150
and a vortex is basically,

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.280
think of it as like a little
bit of air that's swirling.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:22.780
And so it's faster than
the typical air around it,

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:25.040
and you'll remember from, you
know, Bernoulli's principle

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.230
that fast moving air has a pressure drop,

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:29.607
and so that vortex that you
have sitting about the wing

00:09:29.607 --> 00:09:32.020
generates a low pressure region

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:33.650
well over the high
pressure below the wing,

00:09:33.650 --> 00:09:36.960
and that's how the
airplane flies subsonic.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:38.490
Well, it's amazing man, and you know,

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:41.473
thank you so much for your time today,

00:09:41.473 --> 00:09:46.210
and it's exciting to see
how far you guys have come

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:48.070
over these last few years.

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:49.280
Well, thank you, Ashlee.

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:51.549
You've seen it from the very early days.

00:09:51.549 --> 00:09:53.130
It certainly has come a ways.

